id: rust.anyhow-thiserror
kind: rust
title: anyhow vs thiserror
aliases: anyhow, error context, boxed errors
tags: errors, pragmatics
questions:
  - when would you use anyhow instead of a custom error type?
invariant: thiserror for typed, matchable errors at boundaries -- including the status code at an HTTP boundary; anyhow for context-rich, opaque errors inside an application.
answer_30s: Use thiserror when callers must match the error, and anyhow when you only need to report it with context.
answer_2m: |
  The distinction is who consumes the error. Library APIs are consumed by code
  that branches, so they deserve variants; internal plumbing is consumed by a
  human or a log, so it deserves context. anyhow::Error is a boxed trait object
  with a context chain and backtrace, and `.context()` is the idiom that turns
  "No such file" into "cannot read config at /etc/api/config.toml". Using both in
  one binary is normal: anyhow internally, a typed error at the HTTP boundary
  where a status code must be chosen.
when_to_use:
  - application binaries, CLI tools, internal functions
avoid_when:
  - library public APIs, or anywhere a specific status code must be derived
failure_modes:
  - returning anyhow::Error from a library, so every caller loses matchability
  - discarding the source chain when building a response, so logs stop explaining the incident
related:
  - rust.error-enum
  - rust.result
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/anyhow/latest/anyhow/
